    "bio": "his happened right after I graduated high school. After I have graduated from high school, I went on a part-time position as an after-school youth instructor at the Mount Pleasant After School Program near the east side of San Jose two years. During these two years, I always frustrated and impatient with my results on how I managed student's behavior. I always have to deal with the disrespect from the 5th graders, manage my activities to suit the student's wants and needs, and always maintain a calm demeanor even in an emotional saturated disagreement with my supervisor. Incident after incident led to one big memorable experience that I will never forget. During structure play, there was kid that got made fun of by his friends. Discovering that the kid was crying and sad, I came to console him to stand up for himself. I did everything in my power to make sure he was okay and that he feels safe in a school environment but however this kid apparently showed no signs of listening. After structure play was over, I told my supervisor about the incident and just when I was about to tell him what happened, the kid told my supervisor that I told him to stand up for himself. All of a sudden, my supervisor got frustrated with me and told me to \"get out of my site!\" I felt like I have not done anything wrong to get into trouble. Instead of getting sad and angry, I apologized to my supervisor that I will never do it again (even though I have not done anything wrong in a sense). I became more proactive with my approach to everything. Instead of waiting too long for an answer, I ask more questions, I made sure my activities are on point. If I see do not see the results that I want, I made sure to take everything in my own hands to turn the odds. I became more detailed-oriented in the way I handle things with my supervisor and intentional communication to minimized misunderstanding between students. Rather than waiting for problems to dissolve, I take proactive measures to solve my problems in a systematic approach to ensure optimal success in my job. Through this experience, I discovered that success is not about avoiding setbacks but about how I respond to them. Success means showing resilience, learning from criticism—even unfair criticism—and adapting in ways that make me stronger. By the end of my two years, I had grown into someone who could manage conflict calmly, think ahead, and take ownership of outcomes. This lesson continues to shape my actions today. Whenever I face obstacles, I remind myself that success comes from persistence and adaptability, not from perfect circumstances. I now approach challenges as opportunities to grow, and I know that my ability to stay proactive, resilient, and solution-oriented will guide me toward achieving long-term success in both my career and personal life.",
